eriod/Shao-tsung\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Shao-tsung\">Shao-tsung</a></div><div class=\"wst-header-forward-arrow\">\u2192</div></div></div><div class=\"wst-header-notes header&#95;notes searchaux wst-unknown-notes\"><style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14791120\">.mw-parser-output .plainSister{display:block;font-size:93%;line-height:normal;list-style-type:none;list-style-image:none;list-style-position:outside;border:1px solid var(--border-color-interactive--hover,#AAA);float:right;clear:right;margin:0.5ex 0 0.5ex 0.5ex;padding:0;background:var(--background-color-base,#FFFFFF);color:var(--color-base,#202122)}.mw-parser-output .sisicon,.mw-parser-output .dabicon{padding-right:1ex}</style><ul class=\"plainSister\"><li class=\"sisitem\"><span class=\"sisicon\"><span typeof=\"mw:File/Frameless\"><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\"><img alt=\"Sister Projects\" src=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/20px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"18\" height=\"18\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/40px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x\" data-file-width=\"1024\" data-file-height=\"1024\" /></a></span></span><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\">sister projects</a>: <a href=\"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q107367667\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"d:Q107367667\">Wikidata item</a></li></ul></div><div class=\"ws-noexport\" id=\"ws-data\" style=\"speak:none\"><span id=\"ws-article-id\">3649390</span><span id=\"ws-title\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period\">Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period</a>, Volume 2 \u2014 Shao T&#39;ing-ts&#39;ai</span><span id=\"ws-editor\">Arthur W. Hummel</span><span id=\"ws-author\">Tu Lien-ch\u00ea</span></div></div>\n<p><span style=\"display:none\" id=\"dynamic&#95;layout&#95;overrider\">Layout 2</span>\n</p>\n<div class=\"prp-pages-output\" lang=\"en\">\n<p><span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"638\" data-page-number=\"638\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 2.pdf/36\" data-page-index=\"36\" data-page-quality=\"3\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;2.pdf/36\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;36\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span><b>SHAO</b> T'ing-ts'ai \u90b5\u5ef7\u91c7 (original <i>ming</i> \u884c\u4e2d T. \u5ff5\u9b6f, \u5141\u65af), Jan. 29, 1648\u20131711, July 11, historian and philosopher, was a native of Y\u00fc-yao,&#32;<span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"639\" data-page-number=\"639\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 2.pdf/37\" data-page-index=\"37\" data-page-quality=\"3\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;2.pdf/37\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;37\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span>Chekiang. His grandfather, Shao Ts\u00eang-k'o \u90b5\u66fe\u53ef (<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14767791\">.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip{cursor:help;border-bottom:thin dotted cornflowerblue}.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip-nodash{border-bottom:none}</style><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u5b50\u552f,&#32;<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Literary name (Hao, \u865f)\">H.</span>&#160;\u9b6f\u516c,&#32;d. 1658, aged 51 <i>sui</i>), was a pupil of Sh\u00ean Kuo-mo \u6c88\u570b\u6a21 (or \u8b28 T. \u53d4\u5247, \u6c42\u5982, 1575\u20131656), who founded in Y\u00fc-yao the Academy known as the Yao-chiang Shu-y\u00fcan \u59da\u6c5f\u66f8\u9662 I to promote the teachings of Wang Shou-j\u00ean (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Chang_Li-hsiang\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Chang Li-hsiang\">Chang Li-hsiang</a></span>)\u2014Wang also having been a native of that place. His father, Shao Ch\u00ean-hsien \u90b5\u8c9e\u986f (<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u7acb\u592b,&#32;<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Literary name (Hao, \u865f)\">H.</span>&#160;\u9db4\u9592,&#32;d. 1670 aged 45 <i>sui</i>), carried on the tradition as a student and teacher. In 1664 Shao T'ing-ts'ai became a pupil of Han K'ung-tang \u97d3\u5b54\u7576 (<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u4ec1\u7236,&#32;<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Literary name (Hao, \u865f)\">H.</span>&#160;\u907a\u97d3,&#32;d. 1671, aged 73 <i>sui</i>), a local scholar of the Wang school. During 1667-68, he went to Shaohsing to attend the Ch\u00eang-j\u00ean chiang-hui \u8b49\u4eba\u8b1b\u6703, a gathering of scholars called together by <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Huang_Tsung-hsi\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Huang Tsung-hsi\">Huang Tsung-hsi</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] and others. It was there that, in 1668, he first met <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Mao_Ch%27i-ling\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Mao Ch&#39;i-ling\">Mao Ch'i-ling</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] whom he admired and with whom he later corresponded. After he was made a licentiate, in 1669, he competed fourteen times for a higher degree, but never succeeded. In 1694 he was engaged by the magistrate of Y\u00fc-yao to take charge of the Yao-chiang Academy. In 1708 he went to Peking where he lived in the home of Sung Chih (1656\u20131726, see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Sung_Lao\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Sung Lao\">Sung Lao</a></span>), and while there he became a friend of <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Wan_Ching\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Wan Ching\">Wan Ching</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>]. He also corresponded with <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Li_Kung\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Li Kung\">Li Kung</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>], the well-known philosopher of North China.\n</p><p>His historical works, entitled \u6771\u5357\u7d00\u4e8b <i>Tung-nan chi-shih</i> and \u897f\u5357\u7d00\u4e8b <i>Hsi-nan chi-shih</i>, deal with the southern Ming regime. They were printed, each in 12 <i>ch\u00fcan</i>, in the \u90b5\u6b66\u5f90\u6c0f\u53e2\u66f8 <i>Shao-wu Hs\u00fc-shih ts'ung-shu</i> (1884). A collection of his essays, entitled \u601d\u5fa9\u5802\u6587\u96c6 <i>Ss\u016d-fu t'ang w\u00ean-chi</i>, 10 <i>ch\u00fcan</i>, was first printed in 1705, and was reprinted in 1894 in the <i>Shao-hsing hsien-ch\u00eang i-shu</i> (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Wang_Hui-tsu\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Wang Hui-tsu\">Wang Hui-tsu</a></span>). A considerable number of these essays are devoted to biographies of Ming and early Ch\u2018ing philosophers and to the lives of Sung and Ming loyalists. Twelve of them, dealing with economics and government, were printed separately in 1711 under the title \u6cbb\u5e73\u7565 <i>Chih-p'ing l\u00fceh</i>.\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431518\">.mw-parser-output .wst-rule{background-color:currentcolor;color:inherit;width:auto;margin:2px auto 2px auto;height:1px;overflow:hidden}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-left{margin-left:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-right{margin-right:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.__uirule{background-color:#a2a9b1;color:inherit;border:0;margin-top:0.2em;margin-bottom:0.2em}</style><hr class=\"wst-rule wst-rule-left\" style=\"color:inherit;width:40%;\" />\n<p>[1/486/9a; <i>Ss\u016d-fu t'ang w\u00ean-chi</i>; <i>Shao-hsing fu-chih</i> (1792) 53/63a; Yao Ming-ta \u59da\u540d\u9054, \u90b5\u5ff5\u9b6f\u5e74\u8b5c <i>Shao Nien-lu nien-p'u</i> (1930); <i>T'oung Pao</i> (1934), p. 98.]\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431519\">.mw-parser-output .wst-right{text-align:right}</style><div class=\"wst-right\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\">\n<p><span class=\"smallcaps\" style=\"font-variant:small-caps;\">Tu Lien-ch\u00ea</span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<p><br />\n&#32;\n</p>\n</div>\n</div>"},"links":[{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Chang Li-hsiang"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Huang Tsung-hsi"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Li Kung"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Mao Ch'i-ling"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Shao-tsung"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Shao I-ch'\u00ean"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Sung Lao"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Wan Ching"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Wang Hui-tsu"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Arthur W. Hummel"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Tu Lien-ch\u00ea"}],"sections":[],"wikitext":{"*":"{{Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period\n | volume   = 2\n | onlysection = shao t'ing-ts'ai\n | previous = Shao I-ch'\u00ean\n | next     = Shao-tsung\n | from     = 36\n | to       = 37\n | notes    =\n | section_author = Tu Lien-ch\u00ea\n}}{{DEFAULTSORT:Shao T'ing-ts'ai}}"}}}